WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.800
[Music]

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:22.280
[Narrator:] A nation at war sends its best and strongest men to fight.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:28.960
Those who actually go to the front endure hardships and dangers unequaled in any of their past experience.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:34.340
Courage, pride and loyalty to their comrades support them in battle.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:38.160
But there are fierce demands upon their emotional resources.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:47.160
[Artillery firing.]

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.520
Day after day they live in danger.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:01:10.080
[Sounds of artillery, shelling, gunfire]

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:16.640
They see their best friends cut down, shattered by shrapnel, and burned by the fire of the enemy.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:21.860
They see the grinding horror of mutilating death.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.840
Finally these mounting pressures may overwhelm the strongest personalities.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:43.020
These men are the residue of the battle.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:51.440
For every four men wounded, one soldier will become a psychiatric casualty.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:59.540
Such men may be shaking or crying, but more often they are just very tired and dirty and depressed.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:05.040
A few words with them show that they are out of the fight, for a time at least.

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:08.020
They are unnerved and have no initiative.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.120
They're not quitters, but are truly ill.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:17.280
A plan for treating these men was developed in the Mediterranean area.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:23.520
This plan involves treatment at three levels, division, army, and base.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:27.560
The psychiatric casualties filter back to the battalion aid stations.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:32.480
From there they are taken by ambulance, together with the physically sick and the wounded,

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:34.640
back to the division clearing station.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.500
Upon arrival they are promptly segregated.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.980
We have learned that many of these men with neurotic reactions

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:55.220
can recover quickly when the battle situation has been left behind temporarily.

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:02.700
Fundamentally we must depend for this recovery on the patient's own recuperative powers.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:11.080
But these powers can best be exercised away from a hospital atmosphere.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:16.920
Consequently, all so-called exhaustion cases

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:20.780
are taken immediately to the nearby training and rehabilitation center.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:28.540
There they will see no wounded, only men who are actively participating in their own recovery.

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:32.440
They see men being retrained for duty.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:45.100
The emphasis is on showing a man what he can do, rather than on worrying about what he has failed to do.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:51.020
Soon after arrival, each soldier is interviewed briefly by the psychiatrist.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:53.880
Here is a mild anxiety state.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:56.860
[Psychiatrist:] Tell me, soldier, what's your trouble?

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:04.700
[Soldier:] Well, sir, I was up on the line and well, we got into pretty heavy shelling, couple of men got killed.

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:10.920
So, when that happened, I was in charge of the platoon, trying to straighten the men out,

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:15.720
I get them straightened out, another shell came in right alongside of me,

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:20.620
picked me up and threw me over a ways, broke my rifle.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:26.400
When I came down, next thing I know I was out in the forward slope

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:28.940
and a lieutenant had me down slapping me.

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:34.560
I was crying. I don't remember much between the time the shell came in close to me

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.020
and the time Lieutenant [?] got me back on forward slope.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:44.660
I was crying and took me back and had me tagged by the medics, come back here.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:46.620
[Psychiatrist:] How do you feel now?

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:48.960
[Soldier:] Shaky a little inside.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:50.740
[Psychiatrist:] What do you mean?

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:57.780
[Soldier:] It's hard to explain. I might shake a little bit on the outside, but on the inside...

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:00.660
I feel more inside than I do outside.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:04.300
Like a boiling feeling. Just can't explain.

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:06.180
[Psychiatrist:] Any other trouble?

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:09.740
[Soldier:] No, sir. Weak, that's all.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:13.260
[Psychiatrist:] Did you run after you picked yourself up?

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:17.320
[Soldier:] I must have, sir, to land way out there 150 yards ahead of the rest of the company.

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:19.100
I don't know how I got out there otherwise.

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:21.840
I don't remember that very clearly, that part,

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:26.100
that's why the lieutenant had me down and told me we were out ahead of the bunch.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:28.420
[Psychiatrist:] How long have you been in the Army?

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:29.780
[Soldier:] Been in two years, sir.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:32.160
[Psychiatrist:] How long have you been overseas?

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:33.180
[Soldier:] One year, sir.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:35.020
[Psychiatrist:] And in combat?

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:37.240
[Soldier:] About nine months, sir.

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:39.560
[Psychiatrist:] You've been a rifleman all this time?

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:40.440
[Soldier:] Yes, sir.

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:43.520
[Psychiatrist:] Up on the line?

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:44.020
[Soldier:] Yes, sir.

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:47.620
[Psychiatrist:] Ever been wounded? [Soldier:] Yes, sir, I've been wounded twice.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:50.680
[Psychiatrist:] How'd you feel after you first got wounded?

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:56.880
[Soldier:] Well, sir, the first time it was pretty heavy and they carried me right off the line.

00:05:57.560 --> 00:05:58.580
I was a little jumpy.

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:01.600
[Psychiatrist:] You were jumpy when you came back to your outfit?

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:04.720
[Soldier:] Well, sir, after I went back on the line again, I

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:08.760
felt a little more nervous than what I did before I was ever wounded.

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:10.840
[Psychiatrist:] How about the second time?

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:12.840
[Soldier:] The second time was a little worse, sir.

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:15.960
[Psychiatrist:] You noticed your nervousness increasing?

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:18.680
[Soldier:] Yes, sir, ever since I've been wounded.

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:23.280
Every time...first before I was ever wounded, I was all right, I never had any trouble at all.

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:28.240
Then after being wounded once I got a little jumpy, a little more cautious.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:34.160
And then the second time, why, after that I don't feel like I have it anymore like I used to.

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:37.060
[Psychiatrist:] Well, soldier, I think that

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:41.340
you've had a pretty common experience of having a shell land near you.

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:41.920
[Soldier:] Yes, sir.

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:43.940
[Psychiatrist:] Shake you up a little.

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:46.880
Give you more of a fright than anything else.

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:51.920
This panic that you had's sort of a safety valve that we all have.

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:57.520
What we're going to do with you is keep you here for a couple of days, give you something

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:04.740
to make you sleep for a little while, we got some good chow here, let you play some poker with the boys.

00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:07.920
I'll see you a couple of days later. That's all.

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:09.360
[Soldier:] All right, sir, thank you.

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:17.340
[Narrator:] In this first interview a beginning is made in helping the man to orient himself.

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:22.060
In helping him to understand that his experience is neither unique nor hopeless.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.460
He's now given a chance to pull himself together,

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:30.700
resting and lying about for a couple of days, with only moderate sedation.

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:37.600
One of the things which helps him most is the chance to reestablish normal social contacts.

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:45.700
He can enjoy the simple pleasure of writing a letter, or the luxury of a good shave.

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:51.820
He must be up for every meal and the food is the best that can be provided.

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:57.080
A sense of physical well-being is encouraged by filling his hungry stomach.

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:02.360
For unlike the nervous civilian, most of these men have ravenous appetites.

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:10.740
After two such days of relaxation each man is again seen briefly by the psychiatrist.

00:08:13.340 --> 00:08:14.700
[Psychiatrist:] How do you feel now?

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:17.440
[Soldier:] Well sir, I've calmed down a little bit.

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:22.040
Feel a little bit better. Got fairly good sleep last night.

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:24.080
[Psychiatrist:] Have any medicine?

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:24.800
[Soldier:] No, sir.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:26.840
[Psychiatrist:] Slept well without it.  [Soldier:] Yes, sir.

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:30.400
[Psychiatrist:] How do you feel about this place?

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:34.360
[Soldier:] Well, sir, get away from the noise and shell fire, and everything else

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:37.280
and get that tension off, I feel much better.

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:41.460
[Psychiatrist:] I think you're going to be all right in another three or four days.

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:48.080
What we're going to do is put you in the training center where you'll have marches, hikes.

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:51.240
You'll get a chance to get your confidence back.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:53.920
When's the last time you shot your rifle?

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:56.060
[Soldier:] Day before yesterday, sir.

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:58.380
[Psychiatrist:] Well, we'll give you a chance to shoot it,

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:02.360
give you a chance to get out there and get back in shape again.

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:04.760
You'll be warm and you'll be dry.

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:08.260
You'll be going back to duty in about five days.

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:10.180
You'll be okay.

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:17.000
Remember, what you had was just a safety valve, which everyone has, which blows off steam.

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:20.500
I think you were more scared about it than anything else.

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:24.180
Anything else?

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:24.680
[Soldier:] No, sir.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:26.880
[Psychiatrist:] Okay.  [Soldier:] Thank you, sir.

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:33.880
[Narrator:] Those men who are well enough move into nearby pup tents, where they live with other men

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:36.640
as soldiers in training and not as medical patients.

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:44.060
At this stage the men once more are given rifles and field equipment, which must be cared for properly.

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:53.160
They are given exercises regularly to get them back in good physical condition.

00:09:54.900 --> 00:10:01.500
They begin to act and look and feel like combat soldiers once more, as they go through hard, daily battle training.

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:06.980
The ruins of recent battlefields lend realism to reconditioning maneuvers.

00:10:08.780 --> 00:10:12.300
Intelligent leadership by battle-tested officers and noncoms

00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:15.100
contributes greatly to the recovery of these soldiers.

00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:21.740
Here is a situation in which they can prove to themselves how well and able they have again become.

00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:27.420
After four or five days of training, they are seen again by the psychiatrist.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:34.960
[Psychiatrist:] You've been here through the training period. You've had about seven days away from the line.

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:35.460
[Soldier:] Yes, sir.

00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:38.260
[Psychiatrist:] How do you feel now?  [Soldier:] I feel good, sir.

00:10:39.020 --> 00:10:40.080
[Psychiatrist:] Ready to go back? [Soldier:] Yes, sir.

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:42.680
[Psychiatrist:] How'd you like the training?

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:45.640
[Soldier:] The training was all right, sir, I think it helped out quite a bit.

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:47.020
[Psychiatrist:] How are your nerves?

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:48.080
[Soldier:] Steady.

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:51.540
[Psychiatrist:] You feel like you're ready to go up there and do a job? [Soldier:] Yes, sir.

00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:53.120
[Psychiatrist:] Okay, good luck to you, soldier.

00:10:53.260 --> 00:10:54.060
[Soldier:] All right, thank you, sir.

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:58.020
[Narrator:] These men are again combat soldiers.

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:04.560
Normally 40 to 50 percent respond to the treatment which has been shown, and return to full duty.

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:08.240
Of these, 80 percent remain on combat duty

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:12.940
and a majority are rated by their officers as average or better than average soldiers.

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:19.880
They go back to their fighting job as new men who know what they can expect of themselves.

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:26.580
A lesser number, however, are more seriously ill and require a different handling.

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:31.020
This man with a reactive depression is such a soldier.

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:33.760
[Psychiatrist:] What's your trouble?

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:38.680
[Soldier:] I just can't stand seeing people killed.

00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:39.820
[Psychiatrist:] I can't hear you.

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:42.040
[Soldier:] I can't stand seeing people killed.

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:45.360
[Psychiatrist:] Did you see people killed?

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:48.220
[Soldier:] Lots of them.

00:11:48.620 --> 00:11:50.120
[Psychiatrist:] What?  [Soldier:] Lots of them.

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:51.760
[Psychiatrist:] Where did this happen?

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:53.520
[Soldier:] The front.

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:55.620
[Psychiatrist:] Well, what happened up there?

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:05.660
[Soldier:] The men on the forward slope and the Germans start shelling them...

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:10.180
they were trapped, couldn't get out.

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:14.440
[Psychiatrist:] What happened to you?

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:17.720
[Soldier:] I was lucky enough to get under a bridge.

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:27.020
It eventually quieted down and I just went crazy and I ran away.

00:12:28.340 --> 00:12:31.160
[Psychiatrist:] Well, how do you feel about that, leaving your buddies?

00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:33.640
[Soldier:] I should be in the stockade.

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:39.740
That's where I belong.

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:42.120
[Psychiatrist:] Well, how do you feel right now?

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:46.380
[Soldier:] I feel all right.

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:49.620
Nothing wrong with me.

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:52.060
[Psychiatrist:] You say you ran away?

00:12:52.580 --> 00:12:53.100
[Soldier:] Yes.

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:56.860
[Psychiatrist:] How do you feel about that?

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:00.980
[Soldier:] I didn't know what I was doing.

00:13:05.820 --> 00:13:07.000
[Psychiatrist:] What were you afraid of?

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:10.580
[Soldier:] Everything.

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:15.520
[Psychiatrist:] What in particular?

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:24.900
What in particular?

00:13:28.060 --> 00:13:28.560
[Soldier:] Dead.

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:30.140
[Psychiatrist:] What?  [Soldier:] Dead.

00:13:31.100 --> 00:13:31.780
[Psychiatrist:] Dead what?

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:35.360
[Soldier:] Dead people. I can't stand seeing them.

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:36.420
[Psychiatrist:] I can't hear you.

00:13:36.780 --> 00:13:38.760
[Soldier:] I can't stand seeing dead people.

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:40.600
[Psychiatrist:] What does that do to you?

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:44.640
[Soldier:] Scares me.

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:51.600
[Psychiatrist:] Well, dead people can't hurt you.

00:13:53.180 --> 00:13:55.260
[Soldier:] I've been telling myself that for years.

00:13:55.260 --> 00:13:55.760
[Psychiatrist:] What?

00:13:55.960 --> 00:13:59.300
[Soldier:] I've been telling myself that for years, but I can't stand looking at 'em.

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:05.760
[Psychiatrist:] Are you afraid of dying?

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:07.540
[Soldier:] No.

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:13.820
I'm not afraid of dying.

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:15.940
[Psychiatrist:] Are you afraid of getting hurt?

00:14:16.620 --> 00:14:17.120
[Soldier:] No.

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:22.300
[Psychiatrist:] Do you feel you have a job to do up there?

00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:30.100
Well, Bergeron, I think that you'll need a little more treatment.

00:14:31.480 --> 00:14:33.460
You're pretty jittery and jumpy now.

00:14:34.020 --> 00:14:38.360
What I'm going to do is send you back to another hospital where you can get more rest

00:14:39.060 --> 00:14:39.840
and more treatment.

00:14:40.660 --> 00:14:41.700
And you'll be okay.

00:14:42.320 --> 00:14:46.660
Just take a few days, get you rested up and you'll feel a lot better.

00:14:49.620 --> 00:14:53.100
[Narrator:] It is obvious that this man is too ill to respond quickly to treatment.

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:55.860
He is retarded and self-accusatory.

00:14:56.520 --> 00:15:00.180
His present condition, together with his past history of neurotic fears,

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:02.400
leaves little hope of a rapid recovery.

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:11.160
Another group of seriously shaken men is composed of old veterans who have been excellent soldiers,

00:15:11.420 --> 00:15:13.180
but have reached the limit of their endurance.

00:15:14.820 --> 00:15:19.680
Here we see a chronic anxiety state which has been labeled the "old sergeant syndrome."

00:15:22.360 --> 00:15:23.980
[Psychiatrist:] Well, what brought this about?

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:29.200
[Soldier:] Well, shelling I guess.

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:30.880
[Psychiatrist:] Yes.

00:15:32.840 --> 00:15:36.540
[Soldier:] I've been going with it for a quite a long time now without any trouble.

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:40.300
[Psychiatrist:] How long have you been in the Army?

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:44.480
[Soldier:] The Army? 34 months.

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:46.820
[Psychiatrist:] How long have you been overseas?

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:51.260
[Soldier:] 29, 30 months.

00:15:52.460 --> 00:15:55.460
[Psychiatrist:] And were you through the African campaign?

00:15:55.700 --> 00:15:56.200
[Soldier:] Yes, sir.

00:15:57.420 --> 00:15:59.200
[Psychiatrist:] Moliterno crossing? [Soldier:] Yes, sir.

00:15:59.640 --> 00:16:01.140
[Psychiatrist:] Anzio?  [Soldier:] Yes, sir.

00:16:01.600 --> 00:16:03.220
[Psychiatrist:] All up through up 'til here?

00:16:03.580 --> 00:16:04.220
[Soldier:] Up 'til here.

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:06.940
[Psychiatrist:] Ever been in the hospital?
[Soldier:] No, sir.

00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:10.280
[Psychiatrist:] Ever lost any combat time? [Soldier:] No, sir.

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:15.400
[Psychiatrist:] Well, that gives you about 320 days of combat.

00:16:15.700 --> 00:16:16.200
[Soldier:] Yes, sir.

00:16:17.900 --> 00:16:19.480
[Psychiatrist:] Well, what do you think's wrong with you?

00:16:20.520 --> 00:16:25.000
[Soldier:] Sir, I feel I've gone about as far as I can go.

00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:28.600
[Psychiatrist:] Sergeant, do you think that you're sick?

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:35.960
[Soldier:] I'm not sick, no. But I'm just not good anymore.

00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:37.900
I feel I've taken about enough.

00:16:38.680 --> 00:16:41.100
[Psychiatrist:] Well, what happens to you on the line?

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:49.500
[Soldier:] Well, sir, you might say I can't stand the shellin' no more.

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:52.860
The last time up there I broke down.

00:16:53.980 --> 00:16:57.620
I have a section to lead, just no good to 'em.

00:16:58.780 --> 00:17:00.340
[Psychiatrist:] What do you mean you broke down?

00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:07.460
[Soldier:] Well, the last shell we took up there had me crying all night.

00:17:11.460 --> 00:17:12.500
Just couldn't stand it.

00:17:13.400 --> 00:17:16.800
It's just, which way to turn or where else to go.

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:20.880
[Psychiatrist:] Well, do you think you could go back up there and do a job?

00:17:21.640 --> 00:17:23.300
[Soldier:] Well, I have my doubts.

00:17:24.180 --> 00:17:25.720
[Psychiatrist:] Suppose I gave you a direct order?

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:29.440
[Soldier:] Well, I guess there's nothing I can do about that.

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:31.160
[Psychiatrist:] You'd go out?
[Soldier:] Go up.

00:17:33.220 --> 00:17:35.540
[Psychiatrist:] But you think you can't make it, is that it?

00:17:35.540 --> 00:17:36.720
[Soldier:] I'm quite sure of it.

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:41.280
[Psychiatrist:] Could you do a job out of shell-fire?

00:17:42.740 --> 00:17:44.000
[Soldier:] I believe I could.

00:17:45.180 --> 00:17:47.520
[Psychiatrist:] How do you feel about leaving your outfit?

00:17:48.160 --> 00:17:52.680
[Soldier:] Well, I feel bad about it. But I'm no good to 'em, I can't lead 'em.

00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:58.740
I get them killed, get them wounded, just no good.

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:06.720
[Psychiatrist:] You think you can take a job in a quartermaster unit, for example?

00:18:08.140 --> 00:18:09.260
[Soldier:] I believe so, sir.

00:18:11.680 --> 00:18:14.020
[Psychiatrist:] How do you feel right now, this moment?

00:18:16.120 --> 00:18:23.120
[Soldier:] Guess I, I don't feel much...though better than if I was up there.

00:18:24.820 --> 00:18:27.320
[Narrator:] Even the best man can stand only so much.

00:18:29.120 --> 00:18:33.020
In the old veteran, anxiety reactions become chronic and fixed.

00:18:34.820 --> 00:18:37.300
He gets to be emotionally labile in combat,

00:18:37.720 --> 00:18:41.780
loses drive and judgment, and may finally bring disaster to his section.

00:18:43.760 --> 00:18:47.580
Often he will hold on in the line until actually ordered out by his CO.

00:18:49.260 --> 00:18:51.920
He is sick and has lost his effectiveness in combat.

00:18:52.300 --> 00:18:55.060
Though he recovers composure rapidly behind the lines.

00:18:55.880 --> 00:18:57.660
He too must be evacuated.

00:18:59.500 --> 00:19:02.660
[Psychiatrist:] Well, I'm going to send you back to the hospital now.

00:19:03.500 --> 00:19:06.260
Back there you'll be disposed of further.

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:07.580
[Soldier:] Thank you, sir.

00:19:10.700 --> 00:19:14.940
[Narrator:] These men and others too ill to be returned promptly to combat duty

00:19:15.320 --> 00:19:20.380
are evacuated to a centralized N.P. clearing station located in the Army area.

00:19:21.040 --> 00:19:24.880
It is set up as close to the front line as the tactical situation permits.

00:19:26.720 --> 00:19:29.980
This small provisional hospital of 250 beds

00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:33.860
is never handicapped by the rush of surgical emergencies from battle.

00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:42.120
Unlike the evacuation hospitals, all its facilities are reserved for the care of psychiatric cases,

00:19:43.480 --> 00:19:48.140
those cases who might easily become chronic neurotics if they are not treated promptly.

00:19:50.920 --> 00:19:56.060
Here they are assigned to ward tents and as soon as possible they are interviewed by a psychiatrist.

00:19:58.820 --> 00:19:59.480
[Psychiatrist:] Come in, corporal.

00:20:01.860 --> 00:20:05.620
[Narrator:] Here again is the depressed soldier who was interviewed at the T and R center.

00:20:07.860 --> 00:20:09.860
[Psychiatrist:] Tell me something about your trouble, corporal.

00:20:11.800 --> 00:20:16.580
[Corporal:] Biggest trouble is I can't stand seeing people get killed.

00:20:17.940 --> 00:20:19.060
Been that way all my life.

00:20:20.200 --> 00:20:21.740
I can't kill anyone either.

00:20:24.480 --> 00:20:24.980
Tried.

00:20:26.860 --> 00:20:27.560
Can't do it.

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:37.200
And then there's guys gettin' killed around me and I started seeing people dead all around.

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:41.220
It did something to me and I couldn't stand it.

00:20:44.300 --> 00:20:47.600
My last day we were on a forward slope...

00:20:51.340 --> 00:20:52.280
[Psychiatrist:] What happened to you?

00:20:52.820 --> 00:20:55.240
[Corporal:] We got trapped on a forward slope,

00:20:56.700 --> 00:20:58.760
the Germans threw everything they had at us.

00:21:02.600 --> 00:21:05.120
I was lucky enough to get under the bridge.

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:10.000
I just watched them take it.

00:21:17.360 --> 00:21:20.620
I don't know what happened to me, I just started running back.

00:21:23.540 --> 00:21:25.480
[Psychiatrist:] How much combat have you had altogether, corporal?

00:21:28.300 --> 00:21:29.140
[Corporal:] Three days, sir.

00:21:29.560 --> 00:21:31.640
[Psychiatrist:] Three days. Have you always been this way?

00:21:31.980 --> 00:21:37.240
[Corporal:] Yes, sir, I could never see anything or anyone die or anything like that.

00:21:39.280 --> 00:21:43.780
[Narrator:] This soldier has improved considerably since his interview at the T and R center yesterday.

00:21:45.500 --> 00:21:48.480
He is less retarded, more alert and responsive.

00:21:49.060 --> 00:21:50.320
His depression is lifting.

00:21:52.240 --> 00:21:55.500
Rest away from the battlefield, a chance to clean up

00:21:55.820 --> 00:21:59.840
and some chance to discuss his problems have produced the usual improvement.

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:04.200
[Psychiatrist:] And as a child, what sort of person were you?

00:22:05.380 --> 00:22:11.080
[Corporal:] I guess I was just an average child, except I had an inferiority complex.

00:22:13.120 --> 00:22:13.940
Very bad.

00:22:16.140 --> 00:22:17.820
[Psychiatrist:] What do you mean by that, corporal?

00:22:19.720 --> 00:22:29.500
[Corporal:] When I was a child I, I just didn't think I was as good as anyone else.

00:22:29.960 --> 00:22:31.740
I had an inferiority complex.

00:22:33.620 --> 00:22:34.720
[Psychiatrist:] Have you ever gotten over that?

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:35.580
[Corporal:] Yes, sir.

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:42.560
[Psychiatrist:] How do you account for that, is there anybody or anything in your family life

00:22:42.920 --> 00:22:44.120
that made you feel that way?

00:22:44.120 --> 00:22:44.620
[Corporal:] No sir.

00:22:45.860 --> 00:22:49.260
[Psychiatrist:] Were you normal size and strength and so on?  [Corporal:] Yes, sir.

00:22:49.600 --> 00:22:51.200
[Psychiatrist:] Did you have any trouble making friends?

00:22:51.740 --> 00:22:52.840
[Corporal:] That was my trouble.

00:22:53.820 --> 00:22:56.520
Didn't seem to have the personality when I was a child.

00:22:56.840 --> 00:22:58.240
I found some later on.

00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:00.880
[Psychiatrist:] Were you much of an athlete, corporal?

00:23:01.140 --> 00:23:01.640
[Corporal:] No, sir.

00:23:02.580 --> 00:23:05.320
[Psychiatrist:] Did you go out for sports and play with other children?

00:23:06.340 --> 00:23:06.840
[Corporal:] Yes, sir.

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:11.080
[Psychiatrist:] Why did you feel inferior to them, can you account for it?

00:23:11.500 --> 00:23:12.620
[Corporal:] No, sir, I can't.

00:23:13.900 --> 00:23:17.900
[Psychiatrist:] How did you react when you were a child and you saw blood or saw somebody hurt?

00:23:18.760 --> 00:23:19.960
[Corporal:] Couldn't look at it, sir.

00:23:20.080 --> 00:23:21.240
[Psychiatrist:] Even when you were very young?

00:23:21.740 --> 00:23:22.240
[Corporal:] Yeah.

00:23:23.520 --> 00:23:25.660
[Psychiatrist:] And how does it make you feel, do you remember?

00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:27.740
[Corporal:] Numb.

00:23:28.340 --> 00:23:33.780
I couldn't stand seeing anything bad, any bad cuts or anything.

00:23:35.060 --> 00:23:36.360
[Psychiatrist:] Did you ever get into a fight?

00:23:37.200 --> 00:23:37.700
[Corporal:] No, sir.

00:23:38.640 --> 00:23:39.140
[Psychiatrist:] How's that?

00:23:39.920 --> 00:23:43.100
[Corporal:] I never cared to fight, I saw someone killed one time in a fight.

00:23:43.560 --> 00:23:44.440
[Psychiatrist:] Tell me about that.

00:23:46.100 --> 00:23:48.100
[Corporal:] It was an ordinary street fight.

00:23:50.560 --> 00:23:51.420
[Psychiatrist:] Speak up a little bit.

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:53.760
[Corporal:] Just an ordinary street fight, sir.

00:23:54.380 --> 00:23:58.240
One man hit another man and he hit his head on a curb.

00:23:59.480 --> 00:24:01.200
I heard later he had gotten killed.

00:24:08.080 --> 00:24:09.220
[Psychiatrist:] How did that make you feel?

00:24:11.440 --> 00:24:13.320
[Corporal:] Made me feel like I shouldn't fight.

00:24:14.840 --> 00:24:15.960
[Psychiatrist:] How old were you then?

00:24:17.180 --> 00:24:18.260
[Corporal:] About 16.

00:24:21.100 --> 00:24:22.900
[Psychiatrist:] What's your education been, corporal?

00:24:25.480 --> 00:24:26.980
[Corporal:] Did one year of high school, sir.

00:24:27.360 --> 00:24:28.180
[Psychiatrist:] Were you a good student?

00:24:29.940 --> 00:24:30.440
[Corporal:] Average.

00:24:31.220 --> 00:24:32.560
[Psychiatrist:] What sort of work have you done?

00:24:33.360 --> 00:24:34.040
[Corporal:] Salesman.

00:24:34.740 --> 00:24:35.500
[Psychiatrist:] What do you sell?

00:24:36.500 --> 00:24:37.440
[Corporal:] Bakery products.

00:24:39.000 --> 00:24:40.680
[Psychiatrist:] Worked for the same people all the time?

00:24:43.360 --> 00:24:43.860
[Corporal:] No, sir.

00:24:44.100 --> 00:24:46.880
I also worked in a grocery store.

00:24:47.320 --> 00:24:48.160
[Sneezes]

00:24:49.080 --> 00:24:50.660
[Psychiatrist:] You ever have any trouble holding a job?

00:24:51.560 --> 00:24:53.860
[Corporal:] No, sir. I always had the best recommendations.

00:24:55.020 --> 00:24:55.700
[Psychiatrist:] Are you married?

00:24:56.720 --> 00:24:57.220
[Corporal:] No, sir.

00:24:58.100 --> 00:24:59.060
[Psychiatrist:] Got a steady girl?

00:24:59.620 --> 00:25:03.020
[Corporal:] Yes, sir. I was shipped out the day before I was supposed to get married.

00:25:03.620 --> 00:25:04.800
[Psychiatrist:] How did you feel about that?

00:25:06.920 --> 00:25:11.080
[Corporal:] I didn't mind so much, sir. She'll always be there.

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:13.840
[Psychiatrist:] What do you think is wrong with you, corporal?

00:25:15.140 --> 00:25:16.140
[Corporal:] I don't know, sir.

00:25:18.060 --> 00:25:22.480
[Psychiatrist:] Corporal, this is a reaction which, one which occurs very frequently

00:25:22.480 --> 00:25:28.880
in men of combat, and it's made you very uncomfortable, but it's not particularly serious

00:25:29.280 --> 00:25:30.360
and you're going to get over it.

00:25:31.240 --> 00:25:32.860
It won't affect you in civilian life.

00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:38.040
We're going to keep you here for a few days, we'll explain things a little better to you

00:25:38.580 --> 00:25:42.880
and when you understand more about this, you're going to feel a good deal better

00:25:43.720 --> 00:25:47.100
and you're going to be able to do your work a good deal better.

00:25:47.660 --> 00:25:50.380
Nothing's going to get worse, I promise you that.

00:25:51.200 --> 00:25:53.440
And nothing's going to interfere with your future life.

00:25:56.020 --> 00:25:58.400
[Narrator:] Here at the Army N.P. clearing station,

00:25:58.660 --> 00:26:02.180
each patient who is evacuated from the division is re-interviewed.

00:26:03.800 --> 00:26:07.260
Here, as earlier, it is pointed out that his reaction is perfectly natural.

00:26:08.260 --> 00:26:10.820
It is emphasized that this reaction is temporary

00:26:11.280 --> 00:26:15.040
and that it should leave no permanent aftereffects to mar civilian adjustment.

00:26:16.080 --> 00:26:21.840
He is encouraged to start out on a course of normal effective behavior, instead of chronic neuroticism.

00:26:23.680 --> 00:26:27.520
Natural social contacts help these men to find themselves again.

00:26:31.040 --> 00:26:35.220
The pleasure of a hot shower adds to the feeling of a return to an ordered existence.

00:26:40.620 --> 00:26:44.660
Relaxation with other soldiers contributes to a feeling of normality.

00:26:48.760 --> 00:26:52.840
Games, reading, and music are available for those who wish them.

00:26:58.180 --> 00:27:02.960
A substantial number of men who couldn't be returned from the division recover sufficiently

00:27:02.960 --> 00:27:06.560
here at the Army N.P. center to be sent back to the front.

00:27:07.200 --> 00:27:12.040
However, the majority need further treatment and will not return to combat in the near future.

00:27:14.600 --> 00:27:19.640
For example, a few tense uncertain patients cannot start on their road to recovery

00:27:20.100 --> 00:27:25.240
because the specific sources of their anxiety have been obscured in the depths of a hysterical amnesia.

00:27:25.920 --> 00:27:27.480
This patient is such a man.

00:27:28.420 --> 00:27:33.120
He recalls readily his anxiety during the first three days of an attack on the Gothic Line,

00:27:33.580 --> 00:27:35.300
until he was blown out of his foxhole.

00:27:36.200 --> 00:27:41.000
But he can't tell what happened from that time, until he came to in an aid station.

00:27:43.220 --> 00:27:46.460
This man will be given an intravenous injection of a barbiturate,

00:27:46.960 --> 00:27:50.440
which will permit him to relive his combat experiences constructively.

00:27:53.000 --> 00:28:00.200
Pentothal sodium, one half gram in 10 CCs of distilled water, is injected while the patient counts slowly.

00:28:06.140 --> 00:28:08.400
At the point where counting becomes confused,

00:28:08.880 --> 00:28:12.580
the patient will be adequately narcotized and highly suggestible.

00:28:16.020 --> 00:28:17.960
[Soldier:] 8, 9.

00:28:17.960 --> 00:28:29.760
[Soldier:] 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15.

00:28:29.760 --> 00:28:30.480
[Psychiatrist:] Louder.

00:28:30.980 --> 00:28:31.860
[Soldier:] 16.

00:28:33.080 --> 00:28:33.900
[Psychiatrist:] 17.

00:28:34.700 --> 00:28:35.880
[Soldier:] 38.

00:28:37.240 --> 00:28:38.280
[Psychiatrist:] 39.

00:28:46.820 --> 00:28:47.640
[Soldier:]Forty.

00:28:49.580 --> 00:28:51.420
[Psychiatrist:] You're back on the battlefield now.

00:28:52.660 --> 00:28:54.420
What are you doing, what are you doing?

00:28:55.660 --> 00:28:57.800
[Soldier:] Sitting in a hole.

00:28:58.560 --> 00:29:01.720
[Psychiatrist:] What happens then, what happens then, you're in your hole?

00:29:02.140 --> 00:29:03.420
Watch out, those shells are coming.

00:29:04.080 --> 00:29:06.080
[Whistling] Watch out!

00:29:06.440 --> 00:29:06.940
Duck!

00:29:09.880 --> 00:29:10.520
What are you doing?

00:29:10.800 --> 00:29:12.740
You're in your hole now, what's happening?

00:29:13.540 --> 00:29:16.400
[Soldier:] The shells are bursting all around.

00:29:17.480 --> 00:29:18.700
[Psychiatrist:] Yes, go on.

00:29:21.140 --> 00:29:23.140
The shells are coming in, where are you?

00:29:24.420 --> 00:29:27.060
[Soldier:] I'm blown outta my hole.

00:29:27.380 --> 00:29:28.380
[Psychiatrist:] And what happens then?

00:29:29.840 --> 00:29:33.440
[Soldier:] I get in there with my other buddy, but he's dead.

00:29:34.100 --> 00:29:35.080
[Psychiatrist:] What do you do then?

00:29:36.820 --> 00:29:38.960
[Soldier:] I crawl inside of him.

00:29:41.980 --> 00:29:45.880
Until it gets too hot there, I go into another hole.

00:29:49.820 --> 00:29:50.860
[Psychiatrist:] What do you do then?

00:29:52.900 --> 00:30:04.200
[Soldier:] We go uphill and dig in together....ahhh, we dig a hole...

00:30:06.160 --> 00:30:12.600
Ahhh, my head...it goes for my head.

00:30:15.020 --> 00:30:17.940
Those shots you give me for my head.

00:30:17.940 --> 00:30:21.060
[Psychiatrist:] Yes. What happened after you got blown out of the other hole?

00:30:22.940 --> 00:30:23.680
What happened then?

00:30:23.980 --> 00:30:25.520
[Soldier:] We take a ridge.

00:30:27.040 --> 00:30:29.940
Lost a good many men in my company.

00:30:32.280 --> 00:30:33.360
We taking it.

00:30:34.360 --> 00:30:34.860
[Psychiatrist:] Yes.

00:30:35.160 --> 00:30:40.540
[Soldier:] They says "Comrades." Comrades, hell!

00:30:43.100 --> 00:30:46.540
You shoot your gun at your best buddy

00:30:46.540 --> 00:30:48.640
and you wanna be comrades.

00:30:50.980 --> 00:30:55.020
Our comrades, we comraded 'em.

00:30:56.300 --> 00:31:00.740
When they got about 15 feet in front of us, they threw their weapons away.

00:31:00.740 --> 00:31:01.760
Comrade.

00:31:02.820 --> 00:31:07.300
Well that didn't make a goddamn difference to us, we shoot 'em!

00:31:08.940 --> 00:31:18.180
I like to get my hands on that one jerry prisoner back there, in the [?].

00:31:19.360 --> 00:31:21.340
Must have got hit in the arm.

00:31:22.280 --> 00:31:25.860
Then he asked for cigarettes, he asked me...

00:31:28.660 --> 00:31:33.280
Cigarettes. Cigarettes. All yours.

00:31:33.280 --> 00:31:34.680
I wouldn't give him none.

00:31:36.840 --> 00:31:38.640
I'll give you the cigarette.

00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:43.140
Then he wanted to look at our knives.

00:31:46.180 --> 00:31:51.020
They looked at the knife, I pulled it out and showed it to him, he jumped back.

00:31:53.940 --> 00:31:56.140
Ooh, ooh. [?]

00:32:01.200 --> 00:32:04.840
[Psychiatrist:] What happened after you were blown out of that other foxhole and you took the ridge?

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:07.880
Remember, you're back there on the ridge now.

00:32:08.340 --> 00:32:09.320
You're back on the ridge.

00:32:09.600 --> 00:32:10.280
Watch out, shell!

00:32:10.820 --> 00:32:13.020
[Soldier:] Ohhh, ohhhh!

00:32:17.140 --> 00:32:20.940
Get me out of here! Tom. Is that you, Tom?

00:32:21.060 --> 00:32:22.140
[Psychiatrist:] Yes, I'm here.

00:32:28.400 --> 00:32:32.560
[Soldier:] My head has a hole back there.

00:32:33.480 --> 00:32:35.960
That's where it's gonna be, right there.

00:32:39.380 --> 00:32:41.980
[Psychiatrist:] What happened then, after you were on the ridge?

00:32:43.400 --> 00:32:49.580
[Soldier:] A shell came in. Close. Hundred and seven.

00:32:55.120 --> 00:33:00.860
Just missed us, it got me and knocked me down, down in a big hole.

00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:06.020
And a rocket, it rolled back down and got me in the head.

00:33:09.360 --> 00:33:16.700
And it's...and that's why we dug, we had already dug in there.

00:33:16.720 --> 00:33:21.180
We were digging in there and I had a hole there and they blew me out of that hole.

00:33:25.160 --> 00:33:28.900
Sergeant Toms, you here?

00:33:29.000 --> 00:33:29.880
[Psychiatrist:] Yes, I'm here.

00:33:30.960 --> 00:33:32.780
[Soldier:] We make it up the hill.

00:33:32.780 --> 00:33:33.780
[Psychiatrist:] We're doing all right.

00:33:34.180 --> 00:33:35.020
[Soldier:] All right.

00:33:35.240 --> 00:33:36.140
[Psychiatrist:] We're doing fine.

00:33:38.100 --> 00:33:40.520
[Soldier:] How is Bill?  [Psychiatrist:] Bill's all right.

00:33:40.680 --> 00:33:43.240
[Soldier:] You sure?  [Psychiatrist:] Yes.

00:33:43.980 --> 00:33:47.000
[Narrator:] Dramatic precipitation into the patient's battle experiences

00:33:47.300 --> 00:33:49.540
results in the reliving of the forgotten scenes.

00:33:50.460 --> 00:33:55.800
But the therapist often may have to lead the patient back to the subject in order to get a consecutive story.

00:33:58.700 --> 00:34:01.740
[Psychiatrist:] Where did you go after they blew you out of your hole for the third time?

00:34:03.900 --> 00:34:08.640
[Soldier:] They carried me out into a German dugout.

00:34:09.160 --> 00:34:11.800
[Psychiatrist:] You're in the German dugout now, tell me about that.

00:34:13.040 --> 00:34:15.040
[Soldier:] They covered me up with blankets.

00:34:15.620 --> 00:34:16.520
[Psychiatrist:] Are you all right?

00:34:17.720 --> 00:34:20.900
[Soldier:] And they put me, I was too [?].

00:34:22.940 --> 00:34:27.420
[Narrator:] In the process, violent emotions are evoked, frequently accompanied by tears.

00:34:32.000 --> 00:34:36.060
This ab reaction is usual, but not always present in a successful interview.

00:34:41.960 --> 00:34:43.240
[Psychiatrist:] You're in a German dugout.

00:34:43.820 --> 00:34:44.900
You're in a German dugout.

00:34:45.280 --> 00:34:46.200
You're safe now.

00:34:47.200 --> 00:34:48.280
You're safe now.

00:34:48.900 --> 00:34:50.760
Where are they taking you from the dugout?

00:34:51.460 --> 00:34:54.420
Where are you going from the dugout? You're going down a trail.

00:34:55.160 --> 00:34:56.400
You're going down a trail.

00:34:57.040 --> 00:34:58.440
You're coming to the aid station.

00:34:59.260 --> 00:35:00.400
Battalion aid station.

00:35:00.920 --> 00:35:03.480
You're safe, you're all well.

00:35:03.720 --> 00:35:05.680
[Soldier:] The shells are still coming in.

00:35:05.680 --> 00:35:07.720
[Psychiatrist:] The shells. Watch it then!

00:35:08.140 --> 00:35:09.200
[Soldier:] You hear that one?

00:35:10.040 --> 00:35:10.540
[Psychiatrist:] I hear it.

00:35:15.220 --> 00:35:18.080
[Soldier:] You hear that barrage over there?

00:35:19.020 --> 00:35:19.760
[Psychiatrist:] I hear it.

00:35:20.900 --> 00:35:22.720
[Soldier:] Then let's get the hell out of here.

00:35:22.720 --> 00:35:24.320
[Psychiatrist:] All right, we're going out of here.

00:35:24.840 --> 00:35:26.420
We're going to the clearing company.

00:35:26.980 --> 00:35:27.480
[Soldier:] Where?

00:35:27.780 --> 00:35:29.380
[Psychiatrist:] 601st clearing company.

00:35:29.800 --> 00:35:32.040
We're going back there.  [Soldier:] 601st...

00:35:32.040 --> 00:35:33.820
[Psychiatrist:] That's way back and you'll be safe.

00:35:35.340 --> 00:35:36.180
We're going there.

00:35:36.880 --> 00:35:40.380
[Soldier:] How soon are they gonna, will they...

00:35:40.380 --> 00:35:42.560
[Psychiatrist:] We're almost there.

00:35:43.460 --> 00:35:45.560
[Soldier:] Will they send me back up there again?

00:35:45.680 --> 00:35:47.420
[Psychiatrist:] No, you'll be all right.

00:35:47.940 --> 00:35:49.020
They're going to make you better.

00:35:49.580 --> 00:35:51.000
[Soldier:] Can I go back to my house?

00:35:51.020 --> 00:35:52.240
[Psychiatrist:] They're going to make you better.

00:35:52.760 --> 00:35:55.380
First you're going to be all well.

00:35:55.660 --> 00:35:57.360
[Soldier mumbles.]

00:35:57.460 --> 00:36:00.500
[Psychiatrist:] First, don't worry about that, first you gotta get better.

00:36:00.780 --> 00:36:04.500
[Mumbling and groaning]

00:36:06.280 --> 00:36:10.260
[Narrator:] As the patient wakes up he is forced to repeat the newly remembered experiences.

00:36:11.080 --> 00:36:14.220
This ensures that the memories will be available for later treatment.

00:36:17.200 --> 00:36:19.720
Other cases present different therapeutic problems.

00:36:21.880 --> 00:36:23.620
Here is a case of major hysteria.

00:36:30.280 --> 00:36:32.540
[Psychiatrist:] How long haven't you been feeling well?

00:36:50.720 --> 00:36:56.900
It's hard for you to speak, I know, but soon you will be better and you will be able to speak.

00:37:00.360 --> 00:37:04.660
[Narrator:] Pronounced hysterical patterns, such as this dysphonia, are not common.

00:37:06.240 --> 00:37:10.600
And where they do exist, early treatment has prevented the spread of the hysterical reactions

00:37:10.800 --> 00:37:11.900
among other patients.

00:37:15.760 --> 00:37:16.540
[Psychiatrist:] Take a cigarette.

00:37:20.260 --> 00:37:24.140
[Narrator:] You will notice that much anxiety remains evident in this case of hysteria.

00:37:26.040 --> 00:37:31.700
Classical conversion hysteria with complete repression of anxiety is not too frequently seen.

00:37:33.860 --> 00:37:38.540
Even here, however, the symptom so dominates the man that it must be removed

00:37:38.880 --> 00:37:41.600
before progress and treatment of his anxiety is possible.

00:37:44.100 --> 00:37:47.240
Speech disorders in general are very resistant to therapy.

00:37:47.840 --> 00:37:51.940
And this particular case showed no change after three interviews with pentothal.

00:37:54.100 --> 00:37:58.860
It was finally necessary to resort to the older method of applying thoracic stimuli

00:37:58.860 --> 00:38:01.460
to his larynx and using strong suggestion.

00:38:02.800 --> 00:38:09.100
Only then did his voice begin to improve significantly and only then could his basic problem be approached.

00:38:20.040 --> 00:38:22.520
[Psychiatrist:] Are you feeling any better since you came here?

00:38:37.040 --> 00:38:37.900
The same, huh?

00:38:38.560 --> 00:38:39.840
Do you feel frightened?

00:38:52.820 --> 00:38:57.560
You'll feel better after you rest for a while and I'll see you again soon.

00:39:00.160 --> 00:39:03.720
[Narrator:] Some patients are even more disturbed and disorganized than this man.

00:39:05.360 --> 00:39:10.600
For example, here is another type of severe anxiety state, with panic and confusion.

00:39:13.080 --> 00:39:15.500
Such cases have been labeled pseudo psychotic.

00:39:16.360 --> 00:39:20.560
The term emphasizes the temporary nature of the completely disorganized state.

00:39:22.780 --> 00:39:28.480
This rifleman was evacuated from the front after his best friend had been disemboweled by a shell fragment.

00:39:33.740 --> 00:39:35.840
He believes that he is still on the battlefield.

00:39:47.940 --> 00:39:50.920
A noise in the background causes him to grovel on the floor

00:39:51.280 --> 00:39:53.920
in an effort to protect himself from imaginary shells.

00:40:10.440 --> 00:40:14.600
Careful nursing care without sedation will often stop this reaction

00:40:14.800 --> 00:40:17.360
and restore contact with reality in a few days.

00:40:18.080 --> 00:40:23.440
However, packs and electroshock treatments sometimes are necessary to speed recovery.

00:40:25.000 --> 00:40:31.100
Men with this severe a reaction frequently are found to have had pronounced neurotic disabilities in their past.

00:40:34.080 --> 00:40:36.040
Here is another man with such a background.

00:40:36.680 --> 00:40:38.120
He is a chronic neurasthenic

00:40:38.240 --> 00:40:42.840
who developed a brief episode of complete disorganization while actually on the battlefield.

00:40:44.420 --> 00:40:48.120
However, he was able to compose himself rapidly when evacuated to the rear.

00:40:49.800 --> 00:40:55.940
His parents separated when he was two, his childhood was unhappy, and he had numerous minor illnesses.

00:40:57.240 --> 00:40:58.800
In combat he was terrified.

00:40:59.700 --> 00:41:02.520
After four days he developed a confused panic state

00:41:03.000 --> 00:41:06.300
and had to be caught by an aid man before he could be brought in for care.

00:41:08.960 --> 00:41:11.140
[Psychiatrist:] Tell me, why did you come into the hospital?

00:41:11.500 --> 00:41:17.120
[Patient:] I came in the hospital because of my nervous behavior. Got into the shelling and I cracked up.

00:41:17.400 --> 00:41:18.760
[Psychiatrist:] Tell me what you mean by that.

00:41:20.020 --> 00:41:24.720
[Patient:] My nerves just folded up on me, shell landed about two foot from my hole.

00:41:25.580 --> 00:41:31.580
Got up and run, just ran, just couldn't control my nerves.

00:41:32.460 --> 00:41:34.460
[Psychiatrist:] How did you feel when you were nervous?

00:41:35.940 --> 00:41:37.500
[Patient:] Still have a wet [?]

00:41:41.280 --> 00:41:45.500
[Narrator:] It is possible that neither this man, nor the pseudo psychotic seen earlier,

00:41:45.680 --> 00:41:47.540
should have been sent into combat originally.

00:41:48.240 --> 00:41:50.740
And it is impossible to return them to battle now.

00:41:55.680 --> 00:42:01.820
However, it must be remembered that good and able soldiers also lose their grip after long and hard combat.

00:42:02.500 --> 00:42:08.280
Again, we see a chronic anxiety state in another veteran sergeant who can no longer do his job in battle.

00:42:10.180 --> 00:42:13.920
He feels so guilty about this that he even refuses to wear his stripes.

00:42:16.020 --> 00:42:20.940
He has been overseas 18 months and has fought through the African and Italian campaigns.

00:42:21.660 --> 00:42:22.660
He was wounded once.

00:42:25.260 --> 00:42:29.600
[Psychiatrist:] Has anything happened during the past few weeks that's upset you particularly?

00:42:30.260 --> 00:42:30.760
[Veteran:] Yes.

00:42:31.080 --> 00:42:31.860
[Psychiatrist:] What was that?

00:42:33.020 --> 00:42:34.300
[Veteran:] One of my friends was killed.

00:42:35.720 --> 00:42:37.140
[Psychiatrist:] How did you feel about that?

00:42:38.840 --> 00:42:40.040
[Veteran:] Well sir, I could hardly stand it.

00:42:41.500 --> 00:42:42.000
[Psychiatrist:] Why?

00:42:44.380 --> 00:42:48.480
[Veteran:] Well, he was the best friend I had while I was there,

00:42:48.820 --> 00:42:51.740
we'd always been together since we joined the company.

00:42:53.100 --> 00:42:54.360
We were very close friends.

00:42:55.980 --> 00:43:00.300
[Psychiatrist:] Do you have any other friends left in the company or any men who were with you in Africa?

00:43:00.300 --> 00:43:02.020
[Veteran:] No, sir. [Psychiatrist:] You're the last one?

00:43:02.140 --> 00:43:02.800
[Veteran:] Yes, sir.

00:43:03.860 --> 00:43:06.960
[Psychiatrist:] How do you feel about going back to duty if you had to?

00:43:09.400 --> 00:43:14.040
[Veteran:] Well, sir, if I had to, if I had been made to, I guess I'd have to go.

00:43:15.700 --> 00:43:17.660
[Psychiatrist:] How do you feel about facing the men again?

00:43:18.660 --> 00:43:20.060
[Veteran:] I wouldn't go back to my company.

00:43:20.360 --> 00:43:21.000
[Psychiatrist:] Why not?

00:43:22.440 --> 00:43:27.080
[Veteran:] I'd feel ashamed to go back to it, after leaving them once.

00:43:28.000 --> 00:43:29.260
[Psychiatrist:] You'd feel ashamed to face them.

00:43:30.740 --> 00:43:32.480
[Narrator:] This sergeant is through in combat.

00:43:33.220 --> 00:43:39.160
Continued and acute battle fear, wounds, loss of friends, guilt feeling, and extreme fatigue

00:43:39.480 --> 00:43:41.600
finally overwhelm the strongest men.

00:43:43.320 --> 00:43:48.240
However, prompt psychiatric care makes further hospitalization unnecessary for many of them.

00:43:51.680 --> 00:43:55.840
These men on the road to recovery are sent to the Army convalescent hospital.

00:43:57.160 --> 00:44:00.880
Here they are promptly reclassified and sent to a replacement depot.

00:44:04.120 --> 00:44:06.820
At the depot they are assigned to jobs in the rear area.

00:44:07.940 --> 00:44:09.940
They can do a useful job once again.

00:44:10.500 --> 00:44:12.180
They can go on fighting the war.

00:44:12.760 --> 00:44:17.320
And what's more important, in doing so they regain their sense of personal value.

00:44:18.140 --> 00:44:20.840
There can be no more effective occupational therapy.

00:44:23.680 --> 00:44:28.020
Those who need further treatment are sent back to the base section N.P. hospital.

00:44:29.600 --> 00:44:33.100
Most of these men also can be sent finally to the replacement center

00:44:33.380 --> 00:44:35.260
for assignment to jobs in the rear area.

00:44:36.060 --> 00:44:38.620
Only a few are evacuated to the United States.

00:44:41.300 --> 00:44:44.700
Less than 10 percent of all psychiatric battle casualties

00:44:45.120 --> 00:44:48.100
continue to be so ill that they must be sent home as patients.

00:44:49.560 --> 00:44:50.860
Only 10 percent.

00:44:51.640 --> 00:44:56.620
That's only one man in 10 who is not able to continue in a useful job overseas.

00:44:58.300 --> 00:44:59.660
But the significant fact

00:44:59.940 --> 00:45:04.620
is that about half of the psychiatric battle casualties can be sent back to actual combat.

00:45:05.500 --> 00:45:08.120
This contrasts with the early days of the war in Africa.

00:45:08.860 --> 00:45:13.740
Then, little or no psychiatric treatment was available ahead of the base section hospitals,

00:45:14.400 --> 00:45:17.500
from which only two percent could be returned to the fighting front.

00:45:20.120 --> 00:45:24.100
But now, early psychiatric treatment is saving thousands of men.

00:45:24.780 --> 00:45:27.400
Men who can continue the fight today,

00:45:28.880 --> 00:45:31.240
men who will be the healthy citizens of tomorrow.

00:45:31.760 --> 00:45:41.280
[Music]

